      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "why": "Only family with feasible designs so far (54/64) and it owns the entire merged front (21/21), best power_index 4.42. It is the proven backbone of the trade-off curve, so it keeps the largest share. Ranges are centred on the accuracy-enabling region (data_width/n_iter large enough to clear 2^-12) but widened downward to 12/10 so the search can push power_index below 4.42; angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding are left at full registry range because they are cheap accuracy levers that shift the front without changing the schedule."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 4, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "why": "0 feasible so far, but that is almost certainly a range problem, not a capability problem: accuracy depends only on data_width/n_iter/angle_guard/frac_guard/rounding, which are identical across families, and pipelined_m still delivers 1 result/cycle so the 50 MSPS constraint is met for any m. Registering only every m stages removes pipeline flip-flops, which is exactly the lever that can beat pipelined on power_index at equal accuracy. Giving it half the budget with the same accuracy-enabling width/iter window plus the full m=2..8 sweep is the cheapest way to test whether it extends the front to lower power; if it stays infeasible after this, it can be dropped in later rounds."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The merged front is currently 100% pipelined, so the hypervolume is entirely carried by one family and one region of the space. Two things are needed: (1) keep pipelined searching, because it is the only proven feasible family and its front must be mapped over the full width/iter/guard ranges before the run ends; (2) give pipelined_m a genuine, properly-ranged attempt, since its only structural difference from pipelined is fewer pipeline registers at identical throughput and identical bit-level accuracy, which is precisely the direction that lowers power_index. Both families are given the same accuracy-enabling window (data_width 12..20, n_iter 10..20) so any feasible point they find is directly comparable on the power/accuracy trade-off, and the guards/rounding are left wide because they are free accuracy levers. Budget is split evenly (50/50) rather than favouring pipelined, because the front is already saturated with pipelined points and the marginal hypervolume gain is more likely to come from opening a second family than from re-sampling a region we already cover."
}

