      , "why": "sole family carrying the whole feasible front (23 feasible, best power_index 3.49 @ W=16, accuracy 12.3 bits); full W/n_iter/guard ranges let the search push both the low-power corner (W<16) and the high-accuracy tail (accuracy_bits >> 15.9, still far inside max_abs_err <= 2^-12)"},
      {"budget_share": 0.25, "family": "pipelined_m", "ranges": [{"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": 4, "low": 0, "param": "angle_guard"}, {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": 16, "low": 8, "param": "data_width"}, {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": 3, "low": 1, "param": "frac_guard"}, {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": 8, "low": 2, "param": "m"}, {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": 24, "low": 12, "param": "n_iter"}, {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": 1, "low": 0, "param": "rounding"}], "why": "same 1 result/cycle throughput class as pipelined but 0/50 feasible so far; retry seeded/unseeded at low m over full ranges to see whether it contributes a lower-power or higher-accuracy point once constraints are met"}],
    "rationale": "Feasible front exists but is a small patch of the objective space: power_index 3.49..5.89 vs HV reference 40, accuracy_bits 12.3..15.9 vs reference 12, and data_width only 16..20 of the registry's 8..28. Accuracy depends only on (data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding), all shared across families, so widening those ranges maps strictly more of the accuracy axis, while lower data_width / guard / frac_guard probe the low-power corner that the min-power_index selection rule reads. The ridge the spec rewards is therefore the accuracy axis (each point contributes ~ (40 - power) x (acc - 12), and the 15.9-bit point alone accounts for ~133 of the 138.9 HV), so the remaining 100 evals go to front mapping rather than refinement around W=16..18. pipelined_m is kept as a 25% probe because throughput 50 MSPS is reachable for it (best seen 91.1) but nothing feasible landed yet; if it stays infeasible it is dropped, since no other family can hit 50 MSPS: iterative/unrolled_k are multi-cycle (N+3 = 17+ cycles at N=14) and pipelined_m's best throughput already shows the multi-cycle families cannot close a 50 MSPS gap with margin."
  },
  "rationale": "Round 1 found a valid but narrow front: 23/100 feasible, all on pipelined (pipelined_m 0/50 feasible); min power_index 3.49 at data_width=16, n_iter=14, angle_guard=4, frac_guard=1, rounding=round, which satisfies throughput 132 MSPS and 2^-12.26 error. The front spans only power_index 3.49..5.89 against reference 40 and accuracy_bits 12.3..15.9 against reference 12, with data_width 16..20 of 8..28, so most of the accuracy axis (and any sub-16-bit power corner) is unexplored. Hypervolume is dominated by the accuracy tail ((40 - 5.89) x 3.9 bits ≈ 133 of 138.9), so mapping the full ranges of the families on the front is worth far more than local refinement. Ties to the selection rule too: the same mapping is the only way to find a cheaper-than-3.49 feasible point, since data_width < 16 was never sampled."
}

{
  "rationale": "Budget is exhausted (200/200 evals, round 3 of 4) and the front is both feasible and converged. Feasibility is proven: 110 of 200 evaluations meet both constraints, and the selection rule (min power_index) is satisfied by pipelined [data_width=16 n_iter=14 angle_guard=4 frac_guard=1 rounding=round] at power_index=3.49, accuracy_bits=12.3 (max_abs_err=0.000203 <= 0.000244141) and throughput 132 MSPS >= 50. The front spans power_index 3.49..9.6 against an HV reference of 40 and accuracy_bits 12.3..21.6 against a reference of 12, i.e. it already covers the useful trade-off curve well past both reference points, and the round-over-round hypervolume gain is only +1.0%, indicating diminishing returns. The only unexplored family, pipelined_m, produced 0 feasible designs in 50 evals (best accuracy 11.98 bits, max throughput 91.1 MSPS), so adding or widening families offers no route to a better front. Nothing in the registry is unreachable, so infeasible is not warranted; stopping is the correct final decision.",
